.SH "Detailed Description"
.PP 

.SH "`@humanfs/core`"
.PP
by \fRNicholas C\&. Zakas\fP

.PP
If you find this useful, please consider supporting my work with a \fRdonation\fP or \fRnominate me\fP for a GitHub Star\&.
.SH "Description"
.PP
The core functionality for humanfs that is shared across all implementations for all runtimes\&. The contents of this package are intentionally runtime agnostic and are not intended to be used alone\&.

.PP
Currently, this package simply exports the \fRHfs\fP class, which is an abstract base class intended to be inherited from in runtime-specific hfs packages (like \fR@humanfs/node\fP)\&.

.PP
\fBWarning\fP
.RS 4
This project is \fBexperimental\fP and may change significantly before v1\&.0\&.0\&. Use at your own caution and definitely not in production!
.RE
.PP
.SH "Installation"
.PP
.SS "Node\&.js"
Install using your favorite package manager for Node\&.js:

.PP
.PP
.nf
npm install @humanfs/core

# or

pnpm install @humanfs/core

# or

yarn add @humanfs/core

# or

bun install @humanfs/core
.fi
.PP

.PP
Then you can import the \fRHfs\fP and \fRPath\fP classes like this:

.PP
.PP
.nf
import { Hfs, Path } from "@humanfs/core";
.fi
.PP
.SS "Deno"
Install using \fRJSR\fP:

.PP
.PP
.nf
deno add @humanfs/core

# or

jsr add @humanfs/core
.fi
.PP

.PP
Then you can import the \fRHfs\fP class like this:

.PP
.PP
.nf
import { Hfs, Path } from "@humanfs/core";
.fi
.PP
.SS "Browser"
It's recommended to import the minified version to save bandwidth:

.PP
.PP
.nf
import { Hfs, Path } from "https://cdn\&.skypack\&.dev/@humanfs/core?min";
.fi
.PP

.PP
However, you can also import the unminified version for debugging purposes:

.PP
.PP
.nf
import { Hfs, Path } from "https://cdn\&.skypack\&.dev/@humanfs/core";
.fi
.PP
.SH "Usage"
.PP
.SS "\fRHfs\fP Class"
The \fRHfs\fP class contains all of the basic functionality for an \fRHfs\fP instance \fIwithout\fP a predefined impl\&. This class is mostly used for creating runtime-specific impls, such as \fRNodeHfs\fP and \fRDenoHfs\fP\&.

.PP
You can create your own instance by providing an \fRimpl\fP directly:

.PP
.PP
.nf
const hfs = new Hfs({ impl: { async text() {} }});
.fi
.PP

.PP
The specified \fRimpl\fP becomes the base impl for the instance, meaning you can always reset back to it using \fRresetImpl()\fP\&.

.PP
You can also inherit from \fRHfs\fP to create your own class with a preconfigured impl, such as:

.PP
.PP
.nf
class MyHfs extends Hfs {
    constructor() {
        super({
            impl: myImpl
        });
    }
}
.fi
.PP
.SS "\fRPath\fP Class"
The \fRPath\fP class represents the path to a directory or file within a file system\&. It's an abstract representation that can be used even outside of traditional file systems where string paths might not make sense\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
const myPath = new Path(["dir", "subdir"]);
console\&.log(myPath\&.toString());     // "dir/subdir"

// add another step
myPath\&.push("file\&.txt");
console\&.log(myPath\&.toString());     // "dir/subdir/file\&.txt"

// get just the last step
console\&.log(myPath\&.name);           // "file\&.txt"

// change just the last step
myPath\&.name = "file\&.json";
console\&.log(myPath\&.name);           // "file\&.json"
console\&.log(myPath\&.toString());     // "dir/subdir/file\&.json"

// get the size of the path
console\&.log(myPath\&.size);           // 3

// remove the last step
myPath\&.pop();
console\&.log(myPath\&.toString());     // "dir/subdir"

// iterate over the steps
for (const step of myPath) {
    // do something
}

// create a new path from a string
const newPath = Path\&.fromString("/foo/bar");
.fi
